# What a Java developer actually does

## Start with observable behavior
A developer turns a request into a program whose behavior can be checked. For a receipt, “calculate the total” is incomplete: are prices in cents, is tax included, can quantity be zero, and what should invalid input do? Write concrete examples before choosing syntax: 3 items at 250 cents cost 750 cents; no items cost zero; negative quantity is rejected.

## Your first complete program
Save this entire example in Main.java:
```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Receipt total: 750 cents");
    }
}
```
The class groups code. The main method is where execution begins. Braces delimit bodies; the statement ends with a semicolon. Double quotes delimit a String literal. println writes text followed by a line break. Java is case-sensitive: Main and main are different names. Java also has three comment forms the compiler ignores when it reads your source: `// a line comment` runs to the end of its line, `/* a block comment */` can span several lines, and `/** a Javadoc comment */` documents a declaration for tools that generate API pages.

Compile with `javac Main.java`, then run `java Main`. Compilation creates Main.class. The launcher receives a class name, not Main.class. You do not need to understand every keyword yet; preserve the entry-point spelling until methods and classes are taught.

## Practice and evidence
Change the total to 1000 and predict the exact line. Remove the semicolon and read the compiler's file, line number, and caret. Restore it. Remove a letter from println and distinguish an unknown-method error from wrong arithmetic. Keep a notebook of requirement, expected result, actual result, and correction. Printing a correct constant proves this first output task only; later lessons must calculate from varying inputs.

# The edit-compile-test-debug loop

## Debugging is an experiment
Use a repeatable input, an expected result, and a hypothesis. If a total is wrong, first determine whether the data was read incorrectly or the computation was wrong. Random edits destroy evidence and make it difficult to know which change mattered.

Save this complete program:
```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < 3; i++) {
            total += i;
        }
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}
```
The intended total of 1, 2, and 3 is 6; the actual total is 3. Before changing code, list the visited values: i is 1, then 2, then the condition fails at 3. The repair is i <= 3. This is a logic error, so successful compilation cannot detect it.

## Use a breakpoint
Pause at total += i, inspect i and total, step once, and record the changed value. “Step over” executes the current call; “step into” enters it; “step out” finishes the current method. A stack frame shows one method invocation, not the complete heap.

## Regression habit
After the repair, rerun the original example and nearby cases: summing through 0, 1, and 3. Later, turn these observations into automated assertions. When several errors appear, repair the earliest meaningful compiler error first because later diagnostics may be consequences. Keep the smallest input that reproduced a bug as a regression test.

